    "available_at": "2026-11-03T23:07:24.801258+00:00",
    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • SRT Performance Joins the 2026 SEMA Show Featuring the First-Ever Jeep Wrangler Scrambler Concept\nThis Tuned 2027 BMW 3 Series Neue Klasse Lives in Fantasy Land, Do You Like the CGI Makeover?\n10 photos\nPhoto: Instagram | ildar_project\nIt may feel like it’s been a while since the new-generation BMW 3 Series premiered, but it hasn’t, because this model is less than two months old.\nThe unveiling took place at the end of September 2026, and it will also touch down in the United States of America to absolutely no one’s surprise, though you will have to wait a bit for that to happen, because the order book will open in the first quarter of 2027. The official website shows that customers will have to choose between the 330 Sedan, 330 xDrive Sedan, and M350 xDrive sedan versions of the car in our market.\nIf you haven’t heard, the most affordable version starts at $49,900, and the xDrive all-wheel drive system is a $2,000 option. This model has a 2.0-liter TwinPower Turbo inline-six engine, which produces 255 horsepower (259 ps/190 kW) and 295 lb-ft (400 Nm) of torque. The 0 to 60 mph (0 to 97 kph) takes 5.9 seconds, regardless of whether we are talking about the rear-wheel drive or the all-wheel drive configuration.\nAs for the BMW M350 xDrive Sedan, it sits at the very top of the lineup, and will continue to do so until the next-generation M3 arrives. Rumor has it that the new M3 might launch in 2028, so that’s plenty of time for the new M350 to make a statement in the premium compact game for four-door rides. And chances are it will. After all, it is just as fast in a straight-line acceleration as the old base M3.\nPhoto: Instagram | ildar_project It takes just 4.1 seconds to hit 60 mph (97 kph) from a standstill, identical to the 2027 BMW M3 Sedan, which has 473 hp (480 ps/353 kW) to play with from its twin-turbo 3-liter inline-six. The M3 Competition and M3 Competition xDrive complete the sprint in 3.8 and 3.4 seconds, respectively, the spec sheet reveals.\nBMW’s new M350 xDrive comes with 437 horsepower (443 ps/325 kW), and it features a twin-turbocharged 3.0-liter straight-six engine with 48-volt mild-hybrid assistance. The thrust is rated at 428 lb-ft (580 Nm), and pricing for this model kicks off at $65,900. Thus, it undercuts the base M3 by $13,400, as the latter kicks off at $79,300.\nJust so you know, the new-gen 3 Series may indeed be new, but it builds on an updated version of the old model’s CLAR platform. And with this information off our chest, it is time to move on to the pictured car. Actually, make that the rendered car, because it came from @ildar_project on Instagram, and features a body kit that makes it stand out more.\nThis virtually-tuned 2027 BMW M350 xDrive features a new hood, has a sharp splitter attached to the factory bumper, special trim on the fenders, new side skirts, and what seem to be new door mirror casings, too. The rear diffuser with its two vertical brake lights is also new, and extends all the way to the rear wheels. There is also a ducktail spoiler that came from the aftermarket world, as well as what seems to be a carbon fiber trunk lid.\nPhoto: Instagram | ildar_project The car has a white paint finish on the outside, some glossy black elements for contrast, plenty of dry carbon details, and a set of Y-spoke alloys from Vossen. The wheels have a concave look, are much larger than the OEM ones, and fill the arches very nicely with the lowered ground clearance. By the way, those yellow daytime running lights are an OEM feature, not an aftermarket one, in case you forgot.\nIt is obvious that the body kit makes this new-gen BMW 3 Series stand out more, and for what it’s worth, it is on the appealing side of things, this writer thinks. Sure, not everyone may agree, as some would rather have the car bone stock, perhaps sprinkled with some factory M goodies from the options list, but this project is far from being a kitschy proposal. And we reckon it will be appreciated in certain circles.\nAre you a fan of the design? And if you said yes, which I did, do you somehow like the Neue Klasse styling? That would obviously be a no for me, because the vizor-like face makes the new-gen BMW 3 Series (and not only) look confused, and some might easily mistake it for something that is a lot cheaper if it had no BMW roundles, especially on the outside.\nThe interior is not my cup of tea either, because it’s a screen fest that tries to keep the design quite minimalist, and chances are nobody will want the new-gen 3 Series used a few years from now, because it will probably be very much like an old smartphone. And nobody dreams about owning an old smartphone, right? Guess time will tell if I am right or wrong. View this post on Instagram A post shared by Ильдар (@ildar_project)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBMW 3 Series BMW 3 Series 2027 BMW 3 Series bmw m350 xdrive tuning rendering renderings\nAbout the author: Cristian Gnaticov\nAfter a series of unfortunate events put an end to Cristian's dream of entering a custom built & tuned old-school Dacia into a rally competition, he moved on to drive press cars and write for a living. He's worked for several automotive online journals and now he's back at autoevolution after his first tour in the mid-2000s. Full profile\nUP NEXT Someone Paid $2.3 Million for a Ferrari Daytona SP3, Drove 80 Miles, Sold for $18.5 Million",
